---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Treadmills Under $600 (2026): 5 Picks With Real Motors";
const description =
  "The ProForm Carbon TLS (about $559, 2.6 CHP, 18 x 55 in. belt, 12% incline) is the best treadmill under $600; BORGUSI’s $570 model holds 400 lb.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "proform-carbon-tls",
    name: "ProForm Carbon TLS",
    brand: "ProForm",
    category: "Best treadmill under $600 for most homes",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F6VVX36Z?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31+1EXxOMRL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: "/proform-carbon-tls-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "About $559 (Amazon, 21 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":559,"high":559},
    specs: "18 x 55 in. belt · 2.6 CHP (ProForm) · 0–10 mph · 0–12% incline · 300 lb max user · 5 in. LCD · folds",
    pros: [
      "An 18 by 55-inch belt, long enough for walking and easy jogging",
      "Powered 0 to 12% incline with one-touch speed and incline buttons, per the listing",
      "The only machine here with a published continuous motor rating: 2.6 CHP on ProForm’s spec sheet",
      "ProShox cushioning and a hydraulic-assisted fold, per the listing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "iFIT features need a paid Train membership; without one it is a manual treadmill with a 5-inch screen",
      "Only one year on the motor in ProForm’s spec sheet; other ProForm pages differ, so check the terms",
      "A 300 lb limit is lower than the online brands offer at this price",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The safest all-round buy under $600: a known brand, a published motor rating and a 55-inch deck. Buy it if you weigh under about 250 lb.",
    description: "The Carbon TLS is ProForm’s entry treadmill and the most balanced machine in this price band. ProForm’s own spec sheet, checked in November 2026, lists an 18 by 55-inch belt, a 2.6 CHP motor, a 10 mph top speed, 12% maximum incline, a 300 lb user weight capacity, a 5-inch display and a folding frame, with assembled dimensions of 67.5 by 31.6 by 56.2 inches. The Amazon listing agrees on 0 to 10 mph and 0 to 12% incline, and adds ProShox cushioning, speed and incline quick buttons, a device shelf and a hydraulic foot bar for folding. The catch is the subscription. Most of what the listing describes, including trainer-led workouts that change speed and incline for you, heart-rate control and the AI coach, is marked as needing an iFIT Train membership. Without one, you have a sound manual treadmill with a small screen and a tablet shelf. The warranty in ProForm’s spec sheet is 10 years on the frame and one year on the motor, parts and labour; this site has seen ProForm quote the motor term differently on other pages, so confirm it at purchase. ProForm’s 2.6 CHP figure is its own; another source this site checked lists a different rating. Amazon showed it at $559.30 on 21 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "borgusi-20-wide-400lb",
    name: "BORGUSI 20 Inch Wide Folding Treadmill",
    brand: "BORGUSI",
    category: "Highest weight limit under $600",
    badge: "Highest Capacity",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HGR5WWZB?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41pfwWmnhXL._SL500_.jpg","width":400,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $570 (Amazon, 21 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":570,"high":570},
    specs: "20 x 50 in. belt · 4.0 HP · 0.6–10 mph · 15% auto incline (title) · 400 lb max user · 7 in. LCD · folds",
    pros: [
      "A 400 lb weight limit, the highest on this page, per the listing",
      "A 20-inch-wide belt for more side-to-side room",
      "Powered incline and 36 preset programs, per the listing",
      "Arrives 95% pre-assembled with a hydraulic soft-drop fold, per the listing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing contradicts itself on incline: 15 levels in one place, 18 in another",
      "A 50-inch deck is shorter than the 55-inch decks on three other picks",
      "An online-only brand; the bullets we read do not state the warranty length",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The pick for heavier users who want a full-size treadmill with incline for under $600. Check the warranty with the seller before buying.",
    description: "BORGUSI is an online-first brand, and this is its heavy-duty folding treadmill. The Amazon listing gives a 50 by 20-inch anti-slip belt with multi-layer shock absorption, a 400 lb weight capacity, a 4.0 HP motor, a 0.6 to 10 mph speed range, a 7-inch LCD that shows time, speed, distance, calories, incline and pulse from handrail sensors, 36 preset programs, a Bluetooth speaker, a hydraulic soft-drop fold and transport wheels, and says it arrives 95% pre-assembled. The title gives a 15% auto incline, but the listing describes 15 incline levels in one heading and 18 levels in the text beneath it, so we have not printed a level count; ask the seller which applies to the unit you receive. The 4.0 HP figure is not labelled as continuous, so treat it as a peak rating. For a heavier user the case for it is simple: 400 lb and a 20-inch belt for under $600 is a combination no established brand on this page matches. The trade-offs are a 50-inch deck, which suits walking and light jogging better than running, and a warranty we could not confirm from the listing bullets. Amazon showed it at $569.99 on 21 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "merach-auto-incline-55",
    name: "MERACH 15/18% Auto Incline Treadmill (55 in. Belt)",
    brand: "MERACH",
    category: "Best for incline walking under $600",
    badge: "Best for Hill Walking",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GG8TW7P3?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51QDh8cWmZL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: "/merach-treadmills/",
    priceRange: "About $552 (Amazon, 21 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":552,"high":552},
    specs: "18 x 55 in. belt · 3.5 HP brushless · 0.5–8.7 mph · 18 incline levels · 350 lb max user · folds",
    pros: [
      "Eighteen auto-incline levels and three hill modes, including Power Hiking, per the listing",
      "An 18 by 55-inch belt with 12 shock absorbers, per the listing",
      "A 350 lb weight limit",
      "A brushless motor, which the listing says is quieter and longer-lasting than brushed designs",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing does not give a maximum incline percentage for this 55-inch version",
      "Top speed is 8.7 mph on this version, the lowest here, though enough for jogging",
      "One listing covers several versions, so check you are buying the 55-inch deck",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The machine to choose if most sessions will be uphill walks, and the longest-deck option for users up to about 300 lb.",
    description: "MERACH sells a family of auto-incline treadmills on one Amazon listing, and the version we picked is the one with the longer 18 by 55-inch belt. For that version the listing gives a 3.5 HP brushless motor, a 0.5 to 8.7 mph speed range, a 350 lb weight limit, 18 auto-incline levels with three preset hill modes (Steady-State Climb, Power Hiking and Hill Sprints), a 7-layer belt with 12 shock absorbers, a hydraulic fold, an LCD display, heart-rate monitoring and the MERACH app. The listing title says 15/18% incline because it groups two decks: the shorter 47 by 19-inch version tops out at 10 mph and 15%, and the listing notes that the longer deck’s maximum incline may be slightly lower than its T31B1 sibling. It does not give a figure, so neither do we. Even so, 18 powered levels on a 55-inch deck with hill programmes is the most incline-focused package on this page, and the long deck gives room for a lengthened uphill stride. The listing also claims operation below 45 dB, which we could not check. The listing promises 24/7 customer support; our MERACH brand page covers the wider range. Amazon showed this version at $552.49 on 21 November 2026, and the same listing showed other versions from about $380.",
  },
  {
    id: "schwinn-sft4-1",
    name: "Schwinn SFT4.1",
    brand: "Schwinn",
    category: "Longest motor warranty under $600",
    badge: "Longest Warranty",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FC12V6LR?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31JTc6dgqwL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: "/schwinn-treadmills/",
    priceRange: "About $590 (Amazon, 21 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":590,"high":590},
    specs: "20 x 55 in. running area · 0–10 mph · 0–10% motorized incline · 300 lb max user (Schwinn) · 10-yr frame and drive motor",
    pros: [
      "The widest and longest deck here: 20 by 55 inches, with three-zone cushioning",
      "Ten years on the frame and drive motor, per Schwinn (checked November 2026)",
      "Built-in fan, Bluetooth speakers and tablet holder, per the listing",
      "About $109 below Schwinn’s own store price on the day we checked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Schwinn does not publish a motor horsepower rating",
      "Weight limit differs by source: 300 lb at Schwinn, 124 kg (about 275 lb) at a European retailer",
      "A basic three-window LED console with five programs",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The long-term buy: a big deck and a 10-year motor warranty from an established brand, if you can live without a screen.",
    description: "The SFT4.1 is Schwinn’s entry treadmill, and its case rests on two things: deck size and warranty. Schwinn’s US product page, checked in November 2026, lists a 20 by 55-inch running area, speeds up to 10 mph, a 0 to 10% motorized incline, a 300 lb maximum user weight, five built-in programs, assembled dimensions of 70 by 33 by 55 inches, a stowed size of 33.5 by 35 by 63.5 inches and an assembled weight of 180 lb. Its warranty is 10 years on the frame and drive motor, with one year on mechanical parts, electronics and labour, the longest motor cover on this page. The Amazon listing agrees on the running area, speed and incline, and adds three-zone cushioning, a SoftDrop folding system, a tablet holder, a built-in fan, Bluetooth speakers and heart-rate grips. Two figures are missing or unclear. Schwinn does not publish a motor rating; a European retailer listing gives 2.5 HP, but we could not confirm it for the US model. And while Schwinn says 300 lb, the same European listing gives 124 kg (about 275 lb), so plan around the lower figure. Schwinn’s own store showed $699 on 21 November 2026, when Amazon showed $590.",
  },
  {
    id: "sunny-sf-t7718",
    name: "Sunny Health & Fitness SF-T7718",
    brand: "Sunny Health & Fitness",
    category: "Most compact treadmill under $600",
    badge: "Most Compact",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B078NG6896?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41K4pR5wNSL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: "/sunny-health-fitness-treadmills/",
    priceRange: "About $565 (Amazon, 21 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":565,"high":565},
    specs: "20 x 47 in. deck · 2.5 HP · 0.6–9 mph · 265 lb max user · no incline · folds to 10.5 x 29.25 x 63 in.",
    pros: [
      "Stands on end to fold to 10.5 inches deep, per the listing",
      "A 20-inch-wide deck set low to the floor",
      "Slip-resistant rubber handrails and a lubrication reminder every 188 miles, per the listing",
      "Nine programs, a speaker and a USB charging port, per its listing title",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No incline at all",
      "A 265 lb limit, the lowest on this page",
      "A 47-inch deck is short for jogging at longer strides",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a small room or apartment where a treadmill has to disappear between sessions, and for users comfortably under 265 lb.",
    description: "The SF-T7718 is built differently from the other four. Instead of folding its deck up against a console, it uses a low-profile frame that stands on end, and the Amazon listing gives a folded size of 10.5 by 29.25 by 63 inches, slim enough to stand against a wall or slide into a closet. In use, the listing gives a 47 by 20-inch running deck set low to the floor, a 2.5 HP motor (in the listing title), speeds of 0.6 to 9 mph, a 265 lb maximum weight, slip-resistant rubber handrails, shock absorption around the deck, an LCD that shows speed, time, distance, calories, pulse and steps, nine built-in programs and a lubrication reminder every 188 miles. There is no incline, which is the main reason it is not higher on this list; if you want hills, choose the ProForm or MERACH. The low deck is a real advantage for anyone nervous about stepping onto a moving belt, and the 20-inch width is generous at this size. The 265 lb limit means it suits users up to about 215 lb with a sensible margin. Sunny sells it on its own store as well as Amazon; our Sunny Health &amp; Fitness page covers the range. Amazon showed it at $565.00 on 21 November 2026.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Treadmills by Budget", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Treadmills Under $600", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-under-600/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best treadmill under $600?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homes, the ProForm Carbon TLS: an 18 by 55-inch belt, a 2.6 CHP motor by ProForm’s rating, 0 to 12% incline and a 300 lb limit, for about $559 on Amazon on 21 November 2026. Heavier users should look at BORGUSI’s 400 lb, 20-inch-wide model, and incline walkers at MERACH’s 18-level machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you get a good treadmill for $600?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, for walking and jogging. At this price you can get a folding treadmill with an 18 to 20-inch belt, powered incline and a weight limit of 300 lb or more. What $600 does not buy is a deck and motor built for regular running, which usually starts around $800 to $1,000.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best treadmill under $600 for heavy people?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The BORGUSI 20-inch-wide treadmill, which its Amazon listing rates at 400 lb, with a 4.0 HP motor and a 20 by 50-inch belt, for about $570 on 21 November 2026. The MERACH incline treadmill is rated at 350 lb with a longer 55-inch belt. Leave a margin of around 50 lb below any stated limit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which treadmill under $600 has the best incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The MERACH auto-incline treadmill, with 18 powered incline levels and three hill programs on an 18 by 55-inch belt, per its listing. Its listing does not state the maximum percentage for the 55-inch version. ProForm’s Carbon TLS reaches a stated 12% and BORGUSI’s title gives 15%.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a walking pad or a treadmill better for $600?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people with the space, a treadmill. At $600 a treadmill gives you handrails, an 18 to 20-inch belt, powered incline and jogging speeds, where a walking pad is usually limited to about 4 mph and a narrower belt. A walking pad only wins if you need it to slide under a bed or desk.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I run on a treadmill under $600?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can jog on any of the five here, and all reach at least 8.7 mph. For regular running, the 47 to 55-inch decks and modest motor ratings are the limit; runners generally want a 60-inch deck and a continuous motor rating of 3.0 CHP or more, which starts in the under-$1,000 tier.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-21",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              ...(product.amazonUrl ? { url: product.amazonUrl } : {}),
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-treadmills-under-600/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-treadmills-under-600/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "price");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-21",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best treadmill under 600",
      "treadmills under 600",
      "treadmill under $600",
      "best treadmill under 600 dollars",
      "folding treadmill under 600",
      "treadmill with incline under 600",
      "home treadmill under 600",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: ProForm Carbon TLS", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/price/">Treadmills by Budget</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Treadmills Under $600 Real Treadmills, Not Walking Pads</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        The best treadmill under $600 for most homes is the ProForm Carbon TLS: an 18 by 55-inch belt, a 2.6 CHP motor, 12% incline and a 300 lb limit for about $559 on Amazon. BORGUSI’s 20-inch-wide model is the pick for users up to 400 lb, and the four others each solve one problem: hills, warranty or a small room.
      </p>
      <p class="ed-answer"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Best Overall: <a href="#proform-carbon-tls">ProForm Carbon TLS</a>. Highest Capacity: <a href="#borgusi-20-wide-400lb">BORGUSI 20 Inch Wide Folding Treadmill</a>. Best for Hill Walking: <a href="#merach-auto-incline-55">MERACH 15/18% Auto Incline Treadmill (55 in. Belt)</a>.</p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">How we chose: makers’ published specifications and manuals, independent reviews and owner reports, cross-checked. Not tested hands-on.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We may earn a commission when you buy through the links below. It costs you nothing and never decides a ranking. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#proform-carbon-tls"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31+1EXxOMRL._SL500_.jpg" alt="ProForm Carbon TLS" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#borgusi-20-wide-400lb"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41pfwWmnhXL._SL500_.jpg" alt="BORGUSI 20 Inch Wide Folding Treadmill" width="400" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#merach-auto-incline-55"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51QDh8cWmZL._SL500_.jpg" alt="MERACH 15/18% Auto Incline Treadmill (55 in. Belt)" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>ProForm Carbon TLS · BORGUSI 20 Inch Wide Folding Treadmill · MERACH 15/18% Auto Incline Treadmill (55 in. Belt)</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Between $400 and $600 is where a home treadmill stops being a walking pad with ambitions
          and starts being a treadmill: a console on uprights, handrails you can hold, a belt at
          least 18 inches wide and a motor rated at 2.5 horsepower or more. It is also the band where
          Amazon listings are hardest to read, because a $560 machine from a brand with a twenty-year
          service network sits next to a $570 machine from a brand that exists only on Amazon, and
          the second one usually has the bigger numbers in its title. The five treadmills below are
          for general home use (walking most days, some jogging, folding away afterwards) and each
          one has a different job: best overall, highest weight limit, hill walking, longest
          warranty and smallest footprint. Every figure comes from the Amazon listing or the
          maker’s own page, with the date we saw it.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#proform-carbon-tls">ProForm Carbon TLS</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Highest Capacity:</span> <a href="#borgusi-20-wide-400lb">BORGUSI 20 Inch Wide Folding Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best for Hill Walking:</span> <a href="#merach-auto-incline-55">MERACH 15/18% Auto Incline Treadmill (55 in. Belt)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Longest Warranty:</span> <a href="#schwinn-sft4-1">Schwinn SFT4.1</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Most Compact:</span> <a href="#sunny-sf-t7718">Sunny Health & Fitness SF-T7718</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this roundup">
          <h2>In this roundup</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#quick-picks">The short answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">The 5 picks</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-we-chose">How We Chose These Treadmills</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-600-buys">What $600 buys</a></li>
            <li><a href="#specs-that-matter">Specs that matter</a></li>
            <li><a href="#folding-and-space">Space and folding</a></li>
            <li><a href="#walking-or-jogging">Walking vs jogging</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-and-brand">Brand and warranty</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-to-buy">When to buy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
