        <div id="what-600-buys" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Does $600 Buy in a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            At $600 you are buying the first tier of folding treadmills with a proper frame, not a premium
            machine. The jump from $400 to $600 is real, but it is mostly about the deck and the frame rather
            than the motor. Under $400, most of what Amazon sells is a walking pad or a 16-inch-wide folder with
            a belt around 40 to 47 inches long. Between $400 and $600 the belt widens to 18 or 20 inches and,
            on the better machines, lengthens to 55 inches, the frame gets uprights and a console, and powered
            incline becomes normal rather than a premium feature.
          </p>
          <p>
            What $600 does not buy is a deck and motor built for daily running. The usual guidance is a
            60-inch deck and a continuous motor rating of 3.0 CHP or more for regular running, and you will
            not find that combination from an established brand at this price. Every machine here is a
            walking and jogging treadmill. If you plan to run several times a week, our <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $800</a> page is the better starting point, and the <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$1,000 roundup</a> covers the machines with longer decks.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the wider picture of what each price tier gets you, from walking pads to commercial
            machines, see <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much a treadmill costs</a>.
