        <div id="specs-that-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Specs Matter Most Under $600?</h2>
<p>
            Three numbers decide whether a treadmill at this price suits you: belt size, weight limit and
            motor rating, roughly in that order. The rest (programs, speakers, app support) is pleasant but
            rarely the reason a budget treadmill succeeds or fails.
          </p>
          <h3>Belt size</h3>
          <p>
            Width matters more for walking than most people expect. On an 18-inch belt you have room for a
            natural walking stride and arm swing; on 16 inches, many adults find themselves watching their
            feet. Twenty inches, which BORGUSI, Schwinn and Sunny offer here, gives more margin for wider
            frames and for drifting sideways when you are tired. Length matters more for jogging: a longer
            stride needs a longer deck, which is why the 55-inch decks on the ProForm, MERACH and Schwinn
            suit joggers better than the 47 and 50-inch decks on the Sunny and BORGUSI.
          </p>
          <h3>Weight limit</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill worked near its limit wears faster, so the usual advice is to leave a margin of
            around 50 lb between your weight and the stated maximum. That makes the 265 lb Sunny a machine
            for users up to about 215 lb, the 300 lb ProForm and Schwinn suitable up to about 250 lb, and
            the 350 lb MERACH and 400 lb BORGUSI the choices for heavier users. Our <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity guide</a> explains why the margin matters and how makers arrive at the figure.
          </p>
          <h3>Motor rating</h3>
          <p>
            The horsepower in an Amazon title is usually a peak figure, which describes a brief burst rather
            than what the motor can sustain for an hour. A continuous-duty rating (CHP) is the more useful
            number, and of the five machines here only ProForm publishes one: 2.6 CHP on its spec sheet. The
            3.5 and 4.0 HP figures on the MERACH and BORGUSI listings are not labelled as continuous, so treat
            them as a ceiling rather than a promise. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> covers the difference in more depth. For walking and jogging, any of these motors is enough; the
            gap only starts to matter for heavy users at higher speeds and steep inclines, where the motor
            works hardest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Much Space Do These Treadmills Need?</h2>
<p>
            All five fold, but they fold in different ways, and the difference matters more than the
            spec sheet suggests. Four of them fold the usual way: the deck lifts up towards the console and
            locks, with a hydraulic or soft-drop strut to lower it safely. That halves the floor area but
            leaves a tall, upright footprint. The Sunny SF-T7718 is built differently, with a low-profile deck
            that stands on end for storage, which is why it folds to a depth of 10.5 inches.
          </p>
          <p>
            The figures below are the ones the maker or listing publishes. Where a source does not give a
            dimension, the cell says so rather than estimating it.
          </p>
