        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill under $600?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most homes, the ProForm Carbon TLS: an 18 by 55-inch belt, a 2.6 CHP motor by ProForm’s rating, 0 to 12% incline and a 300 lb limit, for about $559 on Amazon on 21 November 2026. Heavier users should look at BORGUSI’s 400 lb, 20-inch-wide model, and incline walkers at MERACH’s 18-level machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you get a good treadmill for $600?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, for walking and jogging. At this price you can get a folding treadmill with an 18 to 20-inch belt, powered incline and a weight limit of 300 lb or more. What $600 does not buy is a deck and motor built for regular running, which usually starts around $800 to $1,000.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill under $600 for heavy people?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The BORGUSI 20-inch-wide treadmill, which its Amazon listing rates at 400 lb, with a 4.0 HP motor and a 20 by 50-inch belt, for about $570 on 21 November 2026. The MERACH incline treadmill is rated at 350 lb with a longer 55-inch belt. Leave a margin of around 50 lb below any stated limit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which treadmill under $600 has the best incline?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The MERACH auto-incline treadmill, with 18 powered incline levels and three hill programs on an 18 by 55-inch belt, per its listing. Its listing does not state the maximum percentage for the 55-inch version. ProForm’s Carbon TLS reaches a stated 12% and BORGUSI’s title gives 15%.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad or a treadmill better for $600?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people with the space, a treadmill. At $600 a treadmill gives you handrails, an 18 to 20-inch belt, powered incline and jogging speeds, where a walking pad is usually limited to about 4 mph and a narrower belt. A walking pad only wins if you need it to slide under a bed or desk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I run on a treadmill under $600?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can jog on any of the five here, and all reach at least 8.7 mph. For regular running, the 47 to 55-inch decks and modest motor ratings are the limit; runners generally want a 60-inch deck and a continuous motor rating of 3.0 CHP or more, which starts in the under-$1,000 tier.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
